  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1106 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nActive Weather:\nThe next chance for active weather arrives Saturday afternoon as\nan upper shortwave and accompanying shortwave boundary drop \nthrough the region. Expect rain chances to increase quickly from \nwest to east by Saturday night, with the entire region expected \n(70-100% chance) to see rainfall by Sunday morning. The rain will \nthen make a fairly quick departure during the day Sunday. Forcing \nwill suffice to bring moderate rainfall amounts to most of the \nregion but lacking instability will inhibit any thunder. \nProbability of exceeding half an inch of rainfall continues to sit\naround 40-70%, and the probability of getting over an inch of \nrainfall sits around 30-40% for areas east and north of the Fox \nValley. Colder air wrapping on backside of this system could \nproduce some brief snowfall for portions of far north-central \nWisconsin along the Upper Peninsula border. Amounts will likely be\nless than an inch, with around 10-20% of an inch of snowfall.\n\nTemperatures:\nUpper ridging to our east will keep the warmer in place the next\ncouple of days, with highs in the upper 50s to middle 60s. Record\nhighs that are in the upper 50s and low 60s Friday and Saturday \nlook to be in jeopardy, especially Green Bay, Wausau, Manitowoc \nand Sturgeon Bay. Several record high minimums look to be set \nSaturday as well, with Friday night lows only dropping into upper \n40s to low 50s, provided it does not cool off too fast before \nmidnight. Much cooler air arrives behind the low pressure system \nnext week with highs tumbling in the 30s to around 40 and lows in \nthe teens and 20s and wind chills in the teens and 20s.",
